<h1 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 2 – Lesson # 2</h1>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> Turn Off the Lights, Please.</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b>
        Reading: Respond to texts with personal reflections.
        Reading: Participate in group discussions based on reading assignments.
        Phonemic Awareness: Decode English graphemes and phonemes using knowledge of word parts, syllabification, and phonemic awareness.
      </p>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b>
        Reading: Can respond to texts about conservation, with personal reflections (e.g., "I think saving water is important.").
        Can participate in group discussions about conservation topics (e.g., "We can recycle more and turn off electronics when not in use.").
        Can recognize and decode simple prefixes and suffixes in words (e.g., identifying "-re" in "recycle" and "-er" in "safer").
      </p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Stage & Description</h3></th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Activities & Teacher Actions</h3></th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Estimated Date and Time</h3></th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><em>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</em></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or images of actions related to conservation (e.g., a hand turning off a light, a person recycling a bottle, a dripping faucet). Students shout out words they remember from Lesson 1. The teacher asks, "What do you see?" and guides students to say "Turn off the lights," "Recycle," "Save water."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces key vocabulary for today's reading activity by holding up flashcards and clearly pronouncing words, emphasizing /sh/ in "shade" and /t/ in "turn off." The teacher points to a picture of a light switch and says, "Turn off the light." Then, points to a picture of a tree providing shade and says, "This is shade." The teacher models reading simple sentences like "Turn off the lights when you leave a room." and "Let's save energy."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of a bright room with the light on during the day) "Do we need to turn on the lights when the sun is bright? Yes or No?" (Expected answer: No)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of a plastic bottle) "Can we throw this in the trash, or can we recycle it?" (Expected answer: Recycle it)</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><em>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!"</em></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short informational text titled "Our Conservation Day!" presented as a simple poster or worksheet with accompanying illustrations. The text includes imperatives (e.g., "Turn off the lights," "Save water"), comparatives (e.g., "easier than"), and superlatives (e.g., "the most important"). It incorporates vocabulary such as 'light', 'energy', 'water', 'recycle', 'turn off', 'save', 'cooler', 'safer', 'solar power'. The text explicitly uses words with prefixes and suffixes like "recycle" and "safer" for phonemic awareness. (Genre: Informational text, Register: Simple and direct, Format: Short paragraph with bullet points).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading short, simple texts about familiar topics to understand general meaning and specific information, and identify simple prefixes/suffixes.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher distributes the text. First, students read the title and look at the pictures.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> The teacher asks, "What is this text about?" (Expected answer: Saving energy/water, conservation).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the text aloud once, modeling fluency and pronunciation.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the text silently.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> Students read sentences based on the text and mark them True (T) or False (F).
              <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">We should turn off the lights when we leave a room. (T/F)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Wasting water is good for the environment. (T/F)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Using solar power is the most difficult way to get electricity. (T/F)</li>
              </ul>
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> Students reread the text and circle words with the prefix "-re" (e.g., recycle) and the suffix "-er" (e.g., safer). The teacher guides them by saying, "Find the word that means 'do again' or 'better'."</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><em>Accuracy focus on Reading</em></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading sentences with imperatives, comparatives, and superlatives to suggest conservation actions and express preferences. Identifying specific vocabulary from the theme. Decoding prefixes and suffixes.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Fill-in-the-Blanks.</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with sentences based on the reading text, but with key vocabulary words missing. Students read each sentence and choose the correct word from a word bank (e.g., recycle, turn off, save, energy, water, easier).
              <br>Example sentences:
              <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">We should ______ off the lights when we leave the room.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Please ______ water by closing the faucet.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Using natural light is ______ than using electricity.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Let's ______ plastic bottles and cans.</li>
              </ul>
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Prefix/Suffix Spotting.</b> Students work individually to read a list of words. They underline the prefix "-re" or the suffix "-er" in each word. The teacher provides examples like "re-use," "clean-er."
              <br>Word List: <em>recycle, safer, redo, cooler, brighter, return, easier.</em>
              <br>After underlining, students practice reading the words aloud.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Reading and Discussion.</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives cards with short conservation statements (e.g., "Use a fan instead of AC, it is cooler," "Turn off the TV when no one is watching," "Saving water is the most important thing to do"). Student A reads a statement. Student B responds with their personal reflection based on the statement, using phrases like "I think saving energy is important because..." or "I prefer turning off appliances, it's easier." They then switch roles. The teacher circulates, listens to their reading and discussions, and provides feedback on pronunciation and content.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><em>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!"</em></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Reading scenarios and writing personal reflections.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students receive a worksheet with three short scenarios about daily life activities in the morning, noon, and night, related to energy and water use. Each scenario is followed by two possible actions (A or B). Students read each scenario and the two actions. They choose the best conservation action by circling it. Then, they write one sentence expressing their preference and a brief personal reflection, using an imperative, a comparative, or a superlative from the lesson.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario Examples:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Morning:</b> It is sunny outside, and you are getting ready for school.
              <br>A) Turn on all the lights. B) Use natural light from the window.
              <br>Your reflection: ____________________________________________________________________</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Noon:</b> You finished drinking water, and the faucet is dripping.
              <br>A) Leave the faucet dripping. B) Close the faucet tightly to save water.
              <br>Your reflection: ____________________________________________________________________</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Night:</b> You are going to bed, and the TV is on in an empty room.
              <br>A) Leave the TV on. B) Turn off the TV.
              <br>Your reflection: ____________________________________________________________________</li>
          </ul>
          Students should aim to write reflections like: "I prefer to use natural light; it is easier than wasting electricity." or "Turn off the TV, it's the most important action to save energy at night." The teacher encourages using vocabulary like 'shade', 'light', 'energy', 'save', 'reduce', 'easier', 'most important'.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><em>Formative assessment strategy</em></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher collects the "Reading Scenarios and Personal Reflections" worksheets from Stage 4.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Reading Comprehension:</b> Student correctly identifies the best conservation action for each scenario. (2 points per scenario)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Personal Reflection:</b> Student's reflection makes sense and relates to the scenario. (1 point per reflection)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Target Language Use:</b> Student uses at least one imperative, comparative, or superlative correctly in their reflections. (1 point per reflection)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> Student uses at least two target vocabulary words correctly (e.g., 'light', 'energy', 'save', 'recycle'). (1 point for overall use)</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can read about how to save energy and water: (Circle: Yes / A little / No)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can say why saving energy is important: (Circle: Yes / A little / No)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One new thing I learned today is: _________________________________________</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was the easiest part of reading about conservation today?</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was one new word or idea you learned about saving energy?</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson? (e.g., specific activities, student engagement, clarity of text). Students were engaged with the scenario-based reading, and the fill-in-the-blanks helped reinforce vocabulary. The phonemic awareness activity was clear and students could identify the word parts.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction)? Some students needed more time to formulate their personal reflections. For the next speaking lesson, I will provide more structured sentence starters for expressing opinions and comparisons to support those who struggle with spontaneous speech. I will also ensure group discussions are facilitated with clear roles.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 3, students will use the conservation actions and personal reflections they read and wrote today to discuss actively in pairs and small groups, practicing their speaking and interaction skills.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>